 ## Overview
-This method automates the design of volume rendering transfer functions (TF) by clustering material boundaries defined in the Lightness-Hue (LH) space, a 2D domain where boundaries appear as distinct blobs rather than overlapping arches [S1]. It solves the problem of manual TF specification by identifying initial clustering elements from histogram peaks, grouping them via hierarchical clustering (using Bhattacharyya similarity) or mean-shift clustering, and mapping the resulting clusters to color and opacity [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard scalar-gradient clustering by utilizing the LH transformation to simplify cluster shapes and reduce classification ambiguities [S1], and from pure statistical methods by incorporating spatial connectivity and boundary path integration [S2, unofficial].
+This method automates the design of volume rendering transfer functions (TF) by clustering material boundaries defined in the Lower-Higher (LH) intensity space, a 2D domain where boundaries appear as distinct blobs rather than overlapping arches [S1]. It solves the problem of manual TF specification by identifying initial clustering elements from histogram peaks, grouping them via hierarchical clustering (using Bhattacharyya similarity) or mean-shift clustering, and mapping the resulting clusters to color and opacity [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard scalar-gradient clustering by utilizing the LH transformation to simplify cluster shapes and reduce classification ambiguities [S1], and from pure statistical methods by incorporating spatial connectivity and boundary path integration [S2, unofficial].
 
